Finite differences provide an independent directional check on a score, not an
automatic bias oracle. For a unit direction $v$, define
$s_v=v^\mathsf T\nabla_\theta\ell(\theta)$ and evaluate the same particle
likelihood program at a fixed symmetric set of perturbations. The three-point
central estimate is
\begin{equation}
 D_2(h;v)=\frac{\ell(\theta+hv)-\ell(\theta-hv)}{2h},
 \qquad D_2(h;v)=s_v+O(h^2),
 \label{eq:fd-three-point}
\end{equation}
and the five-point fourth-order estimate is
\begin{equation}
 D_4(h;v)=\frac{\ell(\theta-2hv)-8\ell(\theta-hv)+8\ell(\theta+hv)-\ell(\theta+2hv)}{12h},
 \qquad D_4(h;v)=s_v+O(h^4).                    \label{eq:fd-five-point}
\end{equation}
For an eleven-point cubic fit, let $k=-5,\ldots,5$ and fit
\begin{equation}
 (\widehat\beta_0,\ldots,\widehat\beta_3)
 =\arg\min_\beta\sum_{k=-5}^{5}
 \left[\widehat\ell(\theta+khv)-\sum_{r=0}^{3}\beta_r(kh)^r\right]^2,
 \qquad D_3(h;v)=\widehat\beta_1.              \label{eq:fd-cubic-fit}
\end{equation}
Write $f(u)=\ell(\theta+uv)$. A bounded third derivative near zero suffices
for the second-order statement in \eqref{eq:fd-three-point}. A bounded fifth
derivative, with $f\in C^5$, suffices for the fourth-order statement in
\eqref{eq:fd-five-point} and for the symmetric cubic fit. Four continuous
derivatives alone do not suffice: $f(u)=\operatorname{sign}(u)|u|^{4+\alpha}$,
$0<\alpha<1$, has four continuous derivatives but gives derivative error
of order $h^{3+\alpha}$ for the five-point rule.

\begin{proposition}[Finite-difference mean squared error]
\label{prop:fd-mse}
Let $\widehat L_j$ be the random log-likelihood estimate at node $j$, with
finite second moments and joint covariance matrix $\Sigma$. For coefficients
$c$ that reproduce a first derivative, put
\begin{equation}
 T_h=\frac1h\sum_j c_j\ell(\theta+jhv)-s_v,\qquad
 B_{N,h}=\frac1h\sum_j c_j\{\mathbb E\widehat L_j-\ell(\theta+jhv)\}.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E\left[\frac1h\sum_jc_j\widehat L_j-s_v\right]^2
 =(T_h+B_{N,h})^2+\frac{c^{\mathsf T}\Sigma c}{h^2}.
                                                        \label{eq:fd-mse}
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The estimator's mean error is $T_h+B_{N,h}$. Its centered part is
$h^{-1}c^{\mathsf T}(\widehat L-\mathbb E\widehat L)$, with variance
$h^{-2}c^{\mathsf T}\Sigma c$. Expanding the squared error, the mixed term
with the centered part has zero expectation. The squared bias still contains
$2T_hB_{N,h}$; the truncation and particle biases need not have the same sign.
\end{proof}
A universal U-shaped error curve does not follow. Shared additive noise
cancels because $\sum_jc_j=0$. A mean-square differentiable coupled random
likelihood can have a bounded derivative variance as $h\downarrow0$.
Independent node noise with nonvanishing variance instead yields the familiar
$h^{-2}$ variance growth. The joint coupling, as well as the stencil, matters.

